Step 3: Explanation of the Risk Classification Rule and 5% VaRJustified CV Risk Rule:Low Risk ($\text{CV} < 10\%$): Price and revenue fluctuations are minor, ensuring predictable operational budgets for the Jinja cooperative.Moderate Risk ($10\% \le \text{CV} < 25\%$): Reflects standard agricultural and fisheries market cyclicality where seasonal demand and supply shifts create moderate variance, but the business remains solvent.High Risk ($\text{CV} \ge 25\%$): Indicates severe market instability where volatility threatens operational continuity and debt servicing.Interpretation of 5% Value-at-Risk (VaR):The 5% VaR represents the lower 5th percentile threshold of annual export revenue generated across the 1,000 simulation paths.If the 5% VaR is, for example, UGX $X$, it signifies with 95% confidence that the cooperative's annual export revenue will not fall below UGX $X$ under normal market volatility. This gives management a defensible, statistically grounded benchmark for financial planning and downside risk mitigation.

